#{[17592186488228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488228 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}-\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "756c31bd-d0b5-518e-8fa8-bf3e64b797d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488228 #edb/ref 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}-\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16} & =-1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1 & =\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(z+5)^{2}}{16} & =(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1 \\\\ (z+5)^{2} & =16((x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1) \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 4\\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488298 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186488342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488298 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "346ce6bc-5f2c-5170-a37c-8daa10ff6d75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488298 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186284469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488333 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186488340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488333 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c766094c-6eab-58b2-b2f3-4c23eb53e41f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488333 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186284468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488340 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186488298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488340 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e921225c-624b-5c95-9668-3ca7e3b9bedb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488340 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186488228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488342 #edb/re